# Normalize all text to LF in history AND force LF in the working tree, so the editor / odinfmt (both
# LF, per .editorconfig and odinfmt.json's `newline_style`) match what git checks out - even when
# core.autocrlf=true, which Git for Windows sets in its system config. eol=lf overrides autocrlf.
#
# Keep this in sync with .editorconfig's `end_of_line` and odinfmt.json's `newline_style`. odinfmt does
# NOT read .editorconfig, so without the odinfmt.json setting it writes CRLF on Windows and re-dirties
# the tree after every `just format` against this LF index.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch is the exception: CRLF is what cmd.exe natively expects, and LF-only batch has known
# failure modes around labels and multi-line blocks. src/build.bat - which builds the yyjson static
# library - was being checked out LF by the rule above; it happens to work, but going LF -> CRLF cannot
# break a batch file while the reverse can.
#
# Nothing needs committing for this. The index keeps LF either way (the clean filter strips CR on the
# way in); `eol=crlf` only changes what the smudge filter writes into the working directory. Git will
# NOT rewrite a file that is already sitting there, so an existing checkout stays LF until the file is
# re-checked-out (`rm src/build.bat && git checkout -- src/build.bat`), and `git status` reads clean
# before and after. `git add --renormalize` is the wrong tool here - it operates on the index, which is
# already correct.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Never normalize these. `text=auto` guesses by looking for a NUL byte in the first 8000 bytes, which
# is a heuristic, not a guarantee - and this repository tracks the prebuilt lib/yyjson.lib and
# lib/yyjson.a, where guessing wrong means silently corrupting a library that then fails at link time
# for no visible reason. Saying so explicitly costs nothing.
*.exe binary
*.pdb binary
*.lib binary
*.dll binary
*.obj binary
*.o binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.a binary
*.bc binary
*.spall binary
